{
  "dom_id": "step-q215477",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{1}{4}-x|<\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$\n{{field-3}}\n$2$",
  "worked_solution": "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{1}{4}-x$ whose distance from zero is less than $\\frac{7}{4}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{4}<\\frac{1}{4}-x<\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{4}<\\frac{1}{4}-x & <\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -2<-x & <\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2>x & >-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}<x<2\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
